# senbonzakura, in the same sealed box its competitors run in.
#
# WHY OUR OWN TOOL GETS A CONTAINER TOO
#
# The isolation exists to protect the card from somebody else's dependency tree, and by that
# argument our own arms did not need one: we wrote the code, and it runs on the host venv. Running
# them there anyway would have left a difference underneath the table that no column showed.
#
# The host venv carries torch 2.13.0+cu130. The benchmark base pins torch 2.5.1+cu124. Two arms on
# two torch builds are two sets of kernels, two sets of numerics, and a KL divergence measured
# against a slightly different baseline. `head-to-head/EQUAL-BUDGET.md` said "Card: same", which was
# true and which quietly meant less than a reader would take it to mean.
#
# WHY 2.5.1, CORRECTED 2026-10-01. This paragraph used to say the base pins that version "because
# that is what the competing tools are pinned to", and **that is false**. Read first-hand from the
# upstream trees: Heretic v1.4.0's `pyproject.toml` deliberately leaves torch unspecified, abliterix
# declares no direct torch dependency at all and takes it transitively, and nothing in either chain
# declares a torch upper bound. No competing tool is pinned to 2.5.1 or to anything else.
#
# The real reason is the one `Dockerfile.tool` gives and it is sound: 2.5 is the floor this
# project's own code enforces (`crashsafe.MIN_TORCH`, because transformers' MoE path imports
# `torch.distributed.tensor.DTensor`), and the cu124 wheels match the driver on the card. So the
# pin is right and one of its two stated reasons was not.
#
# Recorded rather than deleted, because a reader who checked the old claim against either rival's
# metadata would have found it wrong and had no way to tell whether the pin was wrong with it.
#
# So both tools now run on one image, one torch, one CUDA build, with the same flags, the same
# read-only mounts and the same absent network. It also means our own arm is audited by the same
# self-test as everybody else's, which is the answer to a fair question a rival author would ask:
# why is the isolation only applied to other people's code.
#
# WHAT IS BAKED IN, AND WHAT IS NOT
#
# The dependencies are baked; the source is NOT. `head-to-head/run-isolated.sh --senbon-src` mounts the
# working tree read-only at /work/senbon-src, so an arm runs the code that is checked out right
# now and `cli.code_version()` reports it. Baking the source would mean rebuilding the image to
# change one line, and would make the version in the image and the version in the repository two
# things that have to be kept in step by hand.
#
# Build:
#   docker build -f head-to-head/Dockerfile.senbonzakura -t senbon-bench:senbonzakura .
FROM senbon-bench:tool

USER root

# Everything senbonzakura imports, EXCEPT torch, which the base already pins. `--no-deps` is not
# used here: these packages' own dependency trees are wanted, and torch is simply not asked for
# again, so pip has nothing to resolve it against and cannot swap the CUDA build underneath.
#
# Pinned exactly rather than floated, for the same reason the base pins torch: a benchmark whose
# numeric or tokenisation stack moves between arms is not a benchmark. The floors these satisfy
# are in pyproject.toml, with the reason each floor exists. The exact versions are the ones the
# development environment runs, so they are the set the test suite is green against rather than
# whatever a resolver picks on build day.
#
# THE ASSERTION WAS LOAD-BEARING AND TOO NARROW, and this is the correction rather than a tidy-up.
#
# This comment used to end "The torch assertion is the load-bearing line. Any of these packages may
# pull a different torch as a dependency, and it would look like a successful build while silently
# undoing the whole reason this image exists." The diagnosis was right and the scope was wrong:
# the package that actually moved was **numpy**, not torch, and it moved in the sibling image.
#
# `Dockerfile.tool` pinned `numpy==2.1.3`; Heretic v1.4.0 declares `numpy~=2.2`; so the Heretic
# layer upgraded numpy on every build while this image's pins left it alone, and the two arms of a
# published comparison ran on different numpy by construction. A one-package assertion cannot see
# a second package move, whichever package it happens to name.
#
# So numpy is pinned HERE too, at the same version, rather than inherited. This arm does not need
# the upgrade the way Heretic's does, and that is exactly why it has to be written down: an arm
# whose numeric stack is whatever an earlier layer happened to leave behind cannot state its own
# environment, and `CONTRACT.md` presents these arms as one environment.
#
# `--verify-pins` holds every declared pin and every required package from one string, so extending
# the check is editing `BENCH_PINS` or `BENCH_REQUIRED` and not editing an assertion.
ARG TORCH_PIN=2.5.1
ARG NUMPY_PIN=2.4.6
ARG TRANSFORMERS_PIN=5.14.1
ARG ACCELERATE_PIN=1.14.0
ARG DATASETS_PIN=5.0.1
ARG OPTUNA_PIN=4.9.0
# Built up in steps rather than as one line with a backslash continuation inside a quoted default.
# That form is valid and it is also the kind of quoting nobody can check by reading, and this file
# cannot be test-built on the machine it is edited on. Three short lines verify by inspection.
ARG BENCH_PINS_CORE="torch==$TORCH_PIN numpy==$NUMPY_PIN"
ARG BENCH_PINS_STACK="transformers==$TRANSFORMERS_PIN accelerate==$ACCELERATE_PIN"
ARG BENCH_PINS="$BENCH_PINS_CORE $BENCH_PINS_STACK datasets==$DATASETS_PIN optuna==$OPTUNA_PIN"
# `tokenizers` is NOT pinned, and it is named here so the gap is visible rather than absent. It
# arrives with transformers, it decides what the model is shown, and the comment above calls a
# moving tokenisation stack disqualifying. Pinning the whole transitive closure is a separate
# decision (it risks a missing package that only a run finds); requiring it present and recording
# its resolved version is what lands now.
ARG BENCH_REQUIRED="tokenizers safetensors huggingface-hub pyarrow"
ENV BENCH_DECLARED_PINS=$BENCH_PINS \
    BENCH_REQUIRED_PRESENT=$BENCH_REQUIRED

RUN pip install --no-cache-dir \
      "transformers==$TRANSFORMERS_PIN" \
      "accelerate==$ACCELERATE_PIN" \
      "datasets==$DATASETS_PIN" \
      "optuna==$OPTUNA_PIN" \
      "numpy==$NUMPY_PIN" \
 && python /opt/benchenv.py --verify-pins \
 && python -c "import transformers, datasets, optuna, accelerate; print('senbonzakura deps ready')"

# RE-BAKED, because the freeze inherited from the base describes the base and this layer installed
# a dependency tree on top of it.
RUN pip freeze > /opt/bench-env.txt \
 && echo "bench-env.txt: $(wc -l < /opt/bench-env.txt) distributions"

LABEL org.senbonzakura.bench.declared-pins=$BENCH_PINS \
      org.senbonzakura.bench.required-present=$BENCH_REQUIRED \
      org.senbonzakura.bench.freeze="/opt/bench-env.txt"

USER bench
WORKDIR /work

# The source arrives as a read-only mount rather than a layer, so this is where Python looks for
# it. An arm therefore runs the checkout, not a copy of it made at build time.
ENV PYTHONPATH=/work/senbon-src \
    BENCH_TOOL=senbonzakura

CMD ["python", "-c", "import sys; sys.path.insert(0, '/work/senbon-src'); import torch; from senbonzakura import cli; print('senbonzakura', cli.__version__, 'torch', torch.__version__, 'cuda', torch.cuda.is_available())"]
